The held drain needs a few seconds of the attacker's capital genuinely out of the pool (3 s force a level at half
the cap); after it, the freeze costs one transaction a second, as the drag did.  Everything it forces is what the
same LP forces by withdrawing for good (the rows' last column): the frozen L_ema is at or above the depth a
withdrawal for good reaches, L_slow follows the same drained lows, the wrapped liquidation buys at the same
drained depth, and its bound is no looser (a deeper L_b only lowers max_in).  What the attacker saves is keeping
its LP in the pool (its fees) after the held seconds.  The two candidate floors (analysis rules, NOT adopted;
owner item O18) judge the spot leg also at L_mhi -- the upper median of the ring's bucket liquidity highs, the
box's L_hi term: every covered second's high is the keeper's honest depth, so lowering it needs 5 of 8
attacker-only buckets -- or at min(L_mhi, L_slow), which deposit-wrapped cranks cannot raise.  Forced hold levels
after a 20-s held drain; (iv)'s missed keeper seconds (1 h, the open cap and half of it, 3 / 20 misses); their
cost under a capital deposit freeze; and the delay they add to (vi)'s genuine withdrawals, against #101:

